    "q-163548": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $(4-x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=6\\,m/s$ at the origin, find the maximum velocity of the particle.",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14111253/assets/d4e5b78932f8ff6155ac81e12538eb31ac211c9d27d6a2b0c67542527ccd3b2a.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(4-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4-x.\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-x & =0 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=4\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $2$ |  | $6$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $2>0$ | $0$ | $-2<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=4\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4-x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (4-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=6\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(6)^{2} & =4(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+18 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =8x-{x}^{2}+36 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+8x+36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+8x+36}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(4)=\\sqrt{-(4)^{2}+8(4)+36}=2\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s\n$$",
