    "q-41624": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nSuppose that $V=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ Given that $B$ is an orthogonal basis for $V,$ and that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$",
      "worked_solution": "We find an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{8}{8}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the vector ${v}_{3}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{6}{8}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{18}{9}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{3}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 6 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\frac{3}{2},$ $c=\\frac{3}{2},$ and $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{2})}{(\\frac{3}{2})}=-1.$",
