    "q-333680": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 32",
      "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-2y-3,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nTo approximate the solution, one step of the Runge-Kutta method has already been computed, resulting in the following estimate:\n\n$$\ny(1)≈0\n$$\n\nUse the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method with one step to approximate $y(2).$\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*",
      "worked_solution": "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of ${y}^{′}$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{-1}^{′} & =2{x}_{-1}-2{y}_{-1}-3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1-3 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The slope at the current point $(x,y)=(1,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-2y-3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 0-3 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-1)-(-5))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=0+1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =2{x}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{p}-3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1-3 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot (-1)+8\\cdot (-1)-(-5))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =0+(-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{2}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈-\\frac{2}{3}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
